@title Aurora manual failover
@subtitle orders-aurora-prod (Aurora PostgreSQL 16)
@author Database Reliability
@date 2026-10-01

Use this runbook when the writer has been unhealthy for more than 5 minutes and automatic failover has not happened, or for a planned failover in the Sunday window (02:00 to 04:00 UTC).

## Before you start

- [x] You hold the break-glass role `db-failover-operator`
- [x] You have joined #db-incidents
- [ ] The replica lag dashboard is open
-- [ ] Filter to `orders-aurora-prod`
-- [ ] Set the range to the last 15 minutes

## Failover

1. Announce the failover in #db-incidents.
2. Confirm the target reader is healthy.
-- 1. Replica lag is under 30 seconds.
-- 2. CPU is under 70% for the last 5 minutes.
-- 3. No long-running transactions on the reader.
3. Put the orders service into read-only mode with the flag `orders.readonly`.

!!! **Never fail over to a reader whose lag exceeds 30 seconds.** Committed orders will be lost.

4. Trigger the failover to `orders-aurora-prod-r2`.
5. Wait for the cluster endpoint to point at the new writer, usually 30 to 60 seconds.
-- Watch the `writer` field in the console.
-- If nothing changes after 3 minutes, escalate to the DBA lead.
6. Turn `orders.readonly` off and watch connections recover to about 400.

## Verify

table = Check,Expected; Writer instance,`orders-aurora-prod-r2`; Replica lag,under 1 second; Orders API error rate,under 0.5%; Connections,about 400

```bash
aws rds describe-db-clusters --db-cluster-identifier orders-aurora-prod \
  --query 'DBClusters[0].DBClusterMembers[?IsClusterWriter].DBInstanceIdentifier'
```

! If the new writer is unhealthy, fail back to `orders-aurora-prod-r1` with the same steps and page the on-call DBA lead.
